Japanese companies lobbied that, in the pandemic’s pain, a package of salary increases was “unrealistic” News
Keidanren announced the guidelines for upcoming wage negotiations that will end in mid-March, and emphasized that given the current economic and health crisis, the focus is on protecting jobs, not raising wages.
Until last year, as the government put pressure on companies to raise wages to overcome deflation and stagnation, large companies have raised wages by more than 2% each spring for six consecutive years, and deflation and stagnation have plagued the Japanese government. Up to 20 years.
However, in recent years, Japanese companies have begun to adopt more diversified salary methods. In order to avoid attracting young skilled workers, they have avoided full-scale salary increases and switched to performance-based wages instead of seniority-based wages.
An increasing number of low-paid workers tend to lead unions to prioritize work safety and resolve the income gap between long-term employees and other employees, rather than substantially increasing wages. (Reporting by Izumi Nakagawa and Tetsushi Kato; Editing by Huang Biyu)
