Statement: Groundless Charges against Venerable Loun Sovath Need to be Dropped | Cambodia | LICADHO
On March 22, 2009, authorities opened fire on some 80 unarmed villagers from Venerable Sovath’s native Chi Kreng village, who were trying to harvest rice they had planted on land that they had lost in a disputed land deal. Three villagers were wounded, including Venerable Sovath's brother and nephew, while another 12 were imprisoned.
Most notably, Venerable Sovath was awarded the 2012 Martin Ennals Award for Human Rights Defenders on October 2, 2012, becoming the first Cambodian to receive the honor. The award, referred to by many as the “Nobel Prize for human rights defenders”, was created in 1993 to honor and protect individuals who demonstrate exceptional courage in defending and promoting human rights.
Following the trial in 2012, the presiding judge, Leang Samnab, ordered that the case against Venerable Sovath be separated from the case against Serey Ratha, finding insufficient evidence connecting the two individuals. He also stated that there was insufficient evidence to convict Venerable Sovath for incitement.
Disconcertingly, the first of the renewed summons received by Venerable Sovath appeared to indicate that each defendant named in the combined case file would be tried for each of the charges listed therein. After Venerable Sovath’s lawyers requested and were awarded a trial delay on September 18, 2014, the subsequent summons only charge Venerable Sovath with the original incitement charges.
