Chinese authorities seem to be putting things in order rather than declaring war on crypto, aiming to further weaken the U.S. economy.
Related: US-China trade war and its effect on cryptocurrencies
The United States annually inject billions of dollars into the Chinese economy without even suspecting it. The reason is that the majority of Bitcoin (BTC), which is exchanged mainly for U.S. dollars worldwide, is mined in China.
Taking into account China’s share of mining farms, the local miners have earned about $7 billion since last summer. If both the price of Bitcoin and its popularity keep increasing, the revenue will double or even triple each year.
